(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    A002 formalizes an EFFECTIVE ARITHMETIC CERTIFICATE VERIFIER over the A001
    carryless pairing package. Its exported verifier is an executable function
    on natural numbers:

      - `A002_Verify : nat -> nat -> nat`

    The result is always an A001-coded status/payload pair:

      - status `1` carries a numerical certificate payload
      - status `0` carries a numerical error code

    The first implemented kernel, A002-Core, checks finite derivation codes for
    the implicational Hilbert fragment with axiom heads K, S, and modus ponens.
    It verifies a supplied derivation line by line.


2 - State
    -----

                      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                      │              x                   │
                      │   └─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┘ 6 / 10   │
                      └──────────────────────────────────┘

    Evaluation: 6, Working. Effective, mechanized, documented, and connected to
    A001. The executable verifier, parser surface, IO layer, API layer, and
    extraction commands are present.


3 - Intuition
    ---------

    A002 treats proofs as arithmetic data. A derivation is a natural number
    whose A001 decoding exposes a claimed length and a tagged list body:

      ┌────────────┐
      │ derivation │  =  encode n body
      └────────────┘

    The body is not trusted merely because A001 can decode it. Every list node,
    formula node, line, and rule tag must be a canonical A001 pair code before
    A002 uses its components:

      c is structured only if

        canonical001b c = true

      where

        canonical001b c = Nat.eqb (encode (fst001 c) (snd001 c)) c.

    This prevents A002 from silently normalizing malformed numbers through the
    total A001 decoder.

    A line is a canonical pair:

      ┌──────┐
      │ line │  =  encode tag formula
      └──────┘

    The tag selects one of three fixed arithmetic rule checkers:

      tag_axk       checks  A -> (B -> A)
      tag_axs       checks  (A -> (B -> C)) -> ((A -> B) -> (A -> C))
      tag_mp p q    checks  modus ponens from earlier lines p and q

    For modus ponens, line `j` tagged by `tag_mp p q` must cite strictly
    earlier lines:

      p < j,    q < j.

    A002 fetches the displayed formulas at lines `p`, `q`, and `j`, parses the
    formula at `q` as an implication, and checks the purely syntactic alignment:

      line q : A -> B
      line p : A
      line j : B

    Verification is bounded by the claimed length `n`. The main loop runs from
    line `0` through line `n - 1`, carrying a list of numerical local
    certificates. Any failure returns immediately with the first deterministic
    error code:

      reject stage index detail
        = encode 0 (encode stage (encode index detail)).

    If all lines pass, A002 fetches the final displayed formula and compares it
    with the target code `theta` by `Nat.eqb`. On success it returns:

      accept p
        = encode 1 p

    where the certificate payload records the claimed length, target, local
    certificate list, and final formula:

      p = encode n (encode theta (encode certs final_formula)).


